(3412281, 471115, 29168, '<td valign="top">:P Reminds me of my four months stay in the USA. Toilets are designed completely different there you know. (Seriously!) In general, I think the design is probably better -- especially those turbo-charged public versions. But it also leads to the inevitable need for that damned plunger at least once a month it seems -- or perhaps I just need more practice! <br/> <br/>Here (New Zealand), unless a toddler tries to flush Mr. Teddy or something, blockages simply don''t occur. I know no one who owns their own plunger! Yup -- a blocked toilet is a serious matter in these parts! :) Instead, what we get -- and, really only the numorous more poor versions of the design (like my mother''s one for EG.) -- is stuff that just wont go down at all without a helping, umm, stick or brush! And *I* HATE that! *sigh*  TMI? :P<br/> <br/>Oh... nice photo. Love the pose. The harsh light and high contrast works well for the topic. Well done!</td>', ':P Reminds me of my four months stay in the USA. Toilets are designed completely different there you know. (Seriously!) In general, I think the design is probably better -- especially those turbo-charged public versions. But it also leads to the inevitable need for that damned plunger at least once a month it seems -- or perhaps I just need more practice!  Here (New Zealand), unless a toddler tries to flush Mr. Teddy or something, blockages simply don''t occur. I know no one who owns their own plunger! Yup -- a blocked toilet is a serious matter in these parts! :) Instead, what we get -- and, really only the numorous more poor versions of the design (like my mother''s one for EG.) -- is stuff that just wont go down at all without a helping, umm, stick or brush! And *I* HATE that! *sigh*  TMI? :P Oh... nice photo. Love the pose. The harsh light and high contrast works well for the topic. Well done!', '2007-02-28 04:33:00.000000', NULL, 0),
