(3426529, 471362, 57882, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by rdm6265:</b></div><hr/><i> I find the content to be a bit Inappropriate,and somewhat offensive..<br/> </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I take no offense to your post.  As a matter of fact, I value all comments/opinions of my photos.  <br/> <br/>I believe that what an individual takes away from a photo is highly subjective and dependent on that individuals biases, viewpoints, experiences, place in their lives, etc.  What may be enlightening to one way be offensive to another.  Your example of the little girl may be extremely offensive to someone who may have been in that situation as a little girl and experience great hurt.<br/> <br/>So, while I am sincerely sorry that you find the photo offensive, it does not change the fact that others may find it enlightening.  My photos are first and foremost a window to my thoughts, however strong or mild or permanent or fleeting they may be.  Most of my photos are not at all "popular" here at DPC (or anywhere else for that matter), but that does not impact my willingness or intent to continue producing the photos that window my thoughts.<br/> <br/>I am not going to try and detail the exact story behind this particular photo as I intended, because I don''t believe that that is so important.  If five different people look at this photo and see/feel five different visions then I consider that a success.<br/> <br/>So thank you for your post, and please feel free to comment on any of my current or future photos.</td>', 'Originally posted by rdm6265: I find the content to be a bit Inappropriate,and somewhat offensive..   I take no offense to your post.  As a matter of fact, I value all comments/opinions of my photos.   I believe that what an individual takes away from a photo is highly subjective and dependent on that individuals biases, viewpoints, experiences, place in their lives, etc.  What may be enlightening to one way be offensive to another.  Your example of the little girl may be extremely offensive to someone who may have been in that situation as a little girl and experience great hurt. So, while I am sincerely sorry that you find the photo offensive, it does not change the fact that others may find it enlightening.  My photos are first and foremost a window to my thoughts, however strong or mild or permanent or fleeting they may be.  Most of my photos are not at all "popular" here at DPC (or anywhere else for that matter), but that does not impact my willingness or intent to continue producing the photos that window my thoughts. I am not going to try and detail the exact story behind this particular photo as I intended, because I don''t believe that that is so important.  If five different people look at this photo and see/feel five different visions then I consider that a success. So thank you for your post, and please feel free to comment on any of my current or future photos.', '2007-03-03 20:50:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(3426589, 471362, 63398, '<td valign="top">This is just magnificent! You have coalesced the voters into crossing all barriers and awarding an honest look at a truly disturbing part of humanity with the recognition it so deserves.<br/> <br/>I imagine that you had a rough time with your emotions during the course of this challenge and a great feeling of relief to see that so many people put down their different views to join with you in expressing the view that this *IS* hate in its vilest form.<br/> <br/>You are truly insightful and I am privileged to have been here for your sharing of this incredibly poignant shot.<br/> <br/>Many thanks.</td>', 'This is just magnificent! You have coalesced the voters into crossing all barriers and awarding an honest look at a truly disturbing part of humanity with the recognition it so deserves. I imagine that you had a rough time with your emotions during the course of this challenge and a great feeling of relief to see that so many people put down their different views to join with you in expressing the view that this *IS* hate in its vilest form. You are truly insightful and I am privileged to have been here for your sharing of this incredibly poignant shot. Many thanks.', '2007-03-03 21:23:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(3426605, 472130, 27133, '<td valign="top"><b>Hi, Greetings from the Critique Club:</b> <br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b>  I understand the circumstances of which you took this image but I feel the composition could be better.  The out of focus tree and the wire really diminish the photo.  The wire needs to be there but if you had cropped in more the photo would have had more impact.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>  Again the focusing has let you down.  You chose an F4 but that has made the wire more noticeable then it needs to be.  You might not have been able to use flash but a bit of Shadow/Highlight should have helped in this area.<br/> <br/> <br/><b>Overall:</b>  In summary, the image tells a sad story...but the composition and technicalities keeps the viewer at bay from studying the image more.</td>', 'Hi, Greetings from the Critique Club:  Composition:  I understand the circumstances of which you took this image but I feel the composition could be better.  The out of focus tree and the wire really diminish the photo.  The wire needs to be there but if you had cropped in more the photo would have had more impact. Technical:  Again the focusing has let you down.  You chose an F4 but that has made the wire more noticeable then it needs to be.  You might not have been able to use flash but a bit of Shadow/Highlight should have helped in this area.  Overall:  In summary, the image tells a sad story...but the composition and technicalities keeps the viewer at bay from studying the image more.', '2007-03-03 21:33:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(3426726, 471725, 53572, '<td valign="top">a very nice image indeed.  i hdd a little play with it, not basic legal, but i think what i would do with it.  there are two screen layers, on the eyes, with one on the hair and lighter as well, a multiply layer for the foreground fingers, and a high pass layer for the eyes and lighter.  a curves layer at 70% bumped up the contrast a little.<br/> <br/>i hope you don''t mind me fiddling with it.  ya got yourself a looker there!<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=478755" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/53572/thumb/478755.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/53572/thumb/478755.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/53572/thumb/478755.jpg"/></a></td>', 'a very nice image indeed.  i hdd a little play with it, not basic legal, but i think what i would do with it.  there are two screen layers, on the eyes, with one on the hair and lighter as well, a multiply layer for the foreground fingers, and a high pass layer for the eyes and lighter.  a curves layer at 70% bumped up the contrast a little. i hope you don''t mind me fiddling with it.  ya got yourself a looker there! ', '2007-03-03 23:02:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(3426948, 471183, 19831, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>So this is your entry for the ''Hate'' challenge. I think you''ve done a good job of meeting the challenge. The shot has scored well, and congrats on the top 10. I actually have to agree with the majority here, I think you''ve done a real nice job on the image, especially being within the basic ruleset. I think you did fine on the levels, your background looks black to me, no hint of the velvet. The expressions on the faces are very fitting, and definitely send the theme home. I''d also like to note that the upwards lighting can be a great tool, and I think you''ve used it quite well to your advantage here, it works well with the concept of the shot and helps the overall theme. You''ve made a cool image, and it did well in the challenge, I can''t really point out any ''improvements''. One thing that you could possibly consider, not really an improvement at all, but I''m thinking a shot like this, maybe taking the slight offset of the 2 faces, and increasing that offset some, creating more of a diagonal comp, perhaps adding a little bit of dynamics, compositionally. But, maybe not, that could just be me :-). Anyways, nice job on the image, it is pretty cool, and again, congrats. Please feel free to contact me if you have any questions, comments or anything. <br/> <br/>taterbug</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  So this is your entry for the ''Hate'' challenge. I think you''ve done a good job of meeting the challenge. The shot has scored well, and congrats on the top 10. I actually have to agree with the majority here, I think you''ve done a real nice job on the image, especially being within the basic ruleset. I think you did fine on the levels, your background looks black to me, no hint of the velvet. The expressions on the faces are very fitting, and definitely send the theme home. I''d also like to note that the upwards lighting can be a great tool, and I think you''ve used it quite well to your advantage here, it works well with the concept of the shot and helps the overall theme. You''ve made a cool image, and it did well in the challenge, I can''t really point out any ''improvements''. One thing that you could possibly consider, not really an improvement at all, but I''m thinking a shot like this, maybe taking the slight offset of the 2 faces, and increasing that offset some, creating more of a diagonal comp, perhaps adding a little bit of dynamics, compositionally. But, maybe not, that could just be me :-). Anyways, nice job on the image, it is pretty cool, and again, congrats. Please feel free to contact me if you have any questions, comments or anything.  taterbug', '2007-03-04 01:34:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(3426989, 472532, 62318, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Initial Impressions: Great stylized blue portrait.<br/> <br/>Composition: Great use of the entire frame. I like the fact you can see the whole arm. You also created some wonderful lines with the arm and the fingers. The pinky in line with wrinkles with the forehead is terrific. I like how the subject is looking down, but still engaging the viewer. I think I might have preferred a bit more depth in the fingers, yet keeping the body the same. Overall composition is nearly perfect.<br/> <br/>Technicals: Sharpness is great as well as the contrast. Blue tint is perfect on the subject though it seems to bleed a bit on the top. Also isolating the white ( a slight blue tinge) on the bandanna would have added a bit. Maybe a little brightness enhancement in the whites of the eyes. Still technically it is quite good.<br/> <br/>Overall:  A wonderful picture that really evokes the emotion of hate. A nice score for an outstanding picture. Congrats.<br/></td>', '*Critique Club* Initial Impressions: Great stylized blue portrait. Composition: Great use of the entire frame. I like the fact you can see the whole arm. You also created some wonderful lines with the arm and the fingers. The pinky in line with wrinkles with the forehead is terrific. I like how the subject is looking down, but still engaging the viewer. I think I might have preferred a bit more depth in the fingers, yet keeping the body the same. Overall composition is nearly perfect. Technicals: Sharpness is great as well as the contrast. Blue tint is perfect on the subject though it seems to bleed a bit on the top. Also isolating the white ( a slight blue tinge) on the bandanna would have added a bit. Maybe a little brightness enhancement in the whites of the eyes. Still technically it is quite good. Overall:  A wonderful picture that really evokes the emotion of hate. A nice score for an outstanding picture. Congrats.', '2007-03-04 02:19:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(3427004, 472023, 70377, '<td valign="top">Milo you are the Man.... I love this picture and so do my friends in Africa...</td>', 'Milo you are the Man.... I love this picture and so do my friends in Africa...', '2007-03-04 02:52:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(3427047, 472280, 27133, '<td valign="top"><b>Hi, Greetings from the Critique Club:</b> <br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> I feel this image has a great story but it is hidden by the clutter.  We can see there are two firemen and a fire.  The rest of the image doesn''t tell us much...so it doesn''t need to be there.  I have taken the liberty to work on your image as you can see below.  As you can see I have cropped in fairly heavily.  Can you see why though?  Look at the expressions on their faces.  That is great.  You have to realise that this site only allows a 640pixel size.  That isn''t going to show much to the viewer, so you need to show the most obvious.  That expression would just nail this image.  The fire behind links them to the location.  That is all you need.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> The lighting is right, especially considering the heat of the moment.  The timing of the capture is right.  The focus was right.  I did, however find that a nudge of Shadow/Highlight, Contrast and Saturation worked for this image.  Gave it that bit more punch.<br/> <br/><b>Overall:</b>  A great image with loads of potential given the right treatment.  Don''t give up and keep looking for those magical moments.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=478862" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/27133/thumb/478862.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/27133/thumb/478862.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/27133/thumb/478862.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Hi, Greetings from the Critique Club:  Composition: I feel this image has a great story but it is hidden by the clutter.  We can see there are two firemen and a fire.  The rest of the image doesn''t tell us much...so it doesn''t need to be there.  I have taken the liberty to work on your image as you can see below.  As you can see I have cropped in fairly heavily.  Can you see why though?  Look at the expressions on their faces.  That is great.  You have to realise that this site only allows a 640pixel size.  That isn''t going to show much to the viewer, so you need to show the most obvious.  That expression would just nail this image.  The fire behind links them to the location.  That is all you need. Technical: The lighting is right, especially considering the heat of the moment.  The timing of the capture is right.  The focus was right.  I did, however find that a nudge of Shadow/Highlight, Contrast and Saturation worked for this image.  Gave it that bit more punch. Overall:  A great image with loads of potential given the right treatment.  Don''t give up and keep looking for those magical moments. ', '2007-03-04 04:18:13.000000', NULL, 0),
